body=f'''
<header><p class="eyebrow">Vandalizer · Certification audit · 2 October 2026</p><h1>Chat delivery has advanced.<br>The credential needs a 5.0 redesign.</h1><p class="lead">Assessment: <strong>5/10 for curriculum adaptation to Vandalizer 5.0</strong>. The course can be taught through chat, but it still principally certifies the older workflow-builder model. <strong>Release recommendation: hold a “fully updated 5.0 certification” claim</strong> until the entry-path failure, mobile lesson layout, grading contracts and learner-version policy are resolved.</p><p>Reviewed source commit <code>{manifests[0]['commit']}</code>. This is a QA and curriculum review, not an implementation change or a production certification run.</p></header>
<nav><a href="#grades">Grades</a><a href="#findings">Findings</a><a href="#curriculum">Curriculum</a><a href="#migration">Existing learners</a><a href="#delivery">Implementation sequence</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence</a></nav>
<section id="grades"><h2>Grades and what they mean</h2><p>These are human-authored judgments, not automated scores or a measured percentage of migrated code. A 10 means complete, coherent and evidenced for the intended 5.0 learning outcome; 5 means partially adapted with material gaps; 1 means the necessary mechanism is essentially absent.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Grade</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Chat delivery architecture</td><td>7/10</td><td>Seven course tools, five structured card types, shared module progress and chat-specific exercise instructions. Operational acceptance is blocked by the progress-tool exception.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Desktop visual design</td><td>7/10</td><td>Consistent cards, readable grouping, obvious next actions. Contrast, raw markup, small text and competing teaching/challenge content reduce clarity.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mobile visual design</td><td>3/10</td><td>Critical lesson-title collapse at 320/390px; very narrow column. Action buttons can be reached by scrolling.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5.0 curriculum alignment</td><td>5/10</td><td>Real additions, but project scope, approval decisions, recovery, automation and source verification are not a coherent assessed journey.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assessment validity</td><td>3/10</td><td>Artifact counts/configuration often substitute for source-checked outcomes and learner judgment.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Safe course updates for enrolled learners</td><td>1/10</td><td>No pinned curriculum/rubric version, immutable credential issuance or durable shared lesson cursor.</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p><strong>Keep:</strong> research-administration examples, reflective onboarding, shared module credit, reusable lab PDFs, the panel’s optional source-checking walkthrough, source-aware verification lessons, and allowing governance completion on submission rather than examiner availability.</p></section>
<section id="findings"><h2>Prioritized findings</h2><div class="proof-grid"><figure><img src="../../artifacts/visual-review/certification-2026-10-02-interactions/long-top-390.png" alt="Mobile lesson title squeezed into a vertical column of letters"><figcaption>390px: lesson title collapses vertically.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="../../artifacts/visual-review/certification-2026-10-02-run2/check-contradiction-1440.png" alt="Grading card says all checks passed but includes a failed expected-fields check"><figcaption>Passing summary contradicts the failed check.</figcaption></figure></div>{findings}</section>
<section id="curriculum"><h2>How much has actually changed?</h2><p>All <strong>11 modules</strong> have chat-specific exercise instructions. All <strong>74 exported lesson bodies</strong> match the panel source. Four dedicated lessons directly address agentic chat, chat-driven design, trust tiers and guided verification, spread across three modules. That is 4/74 dedicated lessons (5.4%), <em>not</em> a claim that only 5.4% of the curriculum is relevant: many older concepts remain useful. Four of the eight hands-on validators explicitly accept chat-era SearchSet/activity evidence. The rest can use workflows the agent creates, but that does not establish a new learning outcome.</p><p>The important distinction is delivery versus competence. Moving a lesson into a conversation is useful; certifying someone to supervise an agent requires assessing the decisions the person makes around that agent.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>5.0 alignment</th><th>Critique and required update</th></tr></thead><tbody>{module_rows}</tbody></table>
<h3>Recommended 5.0 learning outcomes</h3><ol><li><strong>Orient and scope:</strong> pick the right team/project, distinguish attached documents from knowledge-base and web sources, and confirm what the assistant can access.</li><li><strong>Supervise proposed work:</strong> inspect a proposed extraction/workflow/automation; correct a deliberate mistake; approve or decline a concrete change.</li><li><strong>Verify evidence:</strong> open source passages, correct a wrong field, create grounded test cases, distinguish Unscored, measured quality and examiner approval.</li><li><strong>Recover safely:</strong> handle a failed tool, partial batch and interrupted conversation without duplicating completed changes.</li><li><strong>Deliver and govern:</strong> inspect the output, choose sharing scope, understand ownership and ongoing automation behavior.</li><li><strong>Demonstrate mastery:</strong> complete a short unfamiliar capstone with one wrong proposed action and one bad result; the learner must detect both. Agent assistance is allowed; assessed decisions must belong to the learner.</li></ol><p>Keep the existing 11-module structure initially if that reduces disruption, but rewrite the outcomes and evidence. A later dedicated “Agentic Operator” foundation plus “Workflow Architect” advanced track may fit the product better than requiring all everyday chat users to master advanced nodes.</p></section>
<section id="migration"><h2>Updating the program while people are taking it</h2><p><strong>Recommended policy: preserve earned credit, pin an enrolled learner’s assessed requirements, and offer an explicit 5.0 bridge.</strong> Do not silently change what a completed credential means and do not reset everyone’s progress.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Learner state</th><th>Recommended behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Already certified</td><td>Keep the original credential and completion date. Offer a separate “Vandalizer 5.0 update” bridge. Do not automatically relabel the old credential as 5.0 or remove it when adding requirements.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Started, not certified</td><td>Pin the current enrollment to an immutable legacy course snapshot. Offer “Continue your current course” and “Move to 5.0”; show exactly what carries over and what remains before accepting the move.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mid-lesson, assessment or lab</td><td>Keep the attempt’s lesson/rubric revision and submitted answers intact. Apply migration at a safe checkpoint, never halfway through submission or grading.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Not started / new learner</td><td>Enroll in the published 5.0 version after its acceptance gates pass.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Version unknown in legacy records</td><td>Mark provenance as legacy/unversioned. Recover historical release evidence if available; otherwise do not invent a 4.0/5.0 label. A snapshot taken now preserves the current course but cannot reconstruct what someone saw months ago.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>What can change without requiring a new enrollment?</h3><ul><li><strong>Editorial patch:</strong> typo, broken link, accessibility or layout correction with unchanged objectives and rubric. Publish a patch and record its change note; keep results.</li><li><strong>Optional enrichment:</strong> new example or reference. Label it optional and do not change the completion denominator.</li><li><strong>Material change:</strong> new outcome, mandatory lesson, changed pass/star criteria, exercise dataset or tool behavior that affects assessment. Publish a new course/rubric revision; existing attempts keep their pinned requirements.</li><li><strong>Urgent incorrect guidance:</strong> display a correction immediately and record acknowledgment when needed. If assessment is unsafe or no longer executable, pause only that activity and provide an equivalent replacement; preserve unaffected credit.</li></ul>
<h3>The 5.0 bridge</h3><p>Proposed scope: roughly 45–60 minutes, to be checked with a small learner pilot. Three short units: (1) scope and approval decisions; (2) source verification and quality limits; (3) interruption/partial-failure recovery and safe delivery. Finish with the unfamiliar capstone. Grant equivalent credit for genuinely unchanged outcomes using an explicit mapping; require the new outcomes even for legacy graduates. No duplicate XP for transferred credit.</p>
<h3>Minimum data model</h3><pre>CourseVersion: id, status, product_version, module/lesson IDs,
  content hashes, rubric versions, exercise revisions, change notes
Enrollment: user_id, course_version_id, status, lesson_cursor,
  started_at, migrated_from, migration_choice_at
ModuleAttempt: enrollment_id, module_id, lesson/rubric revision,
  lab/artifact/run IDs, source evidence, answers, grade, timestamps
CreditTransfer: from enrollment/attempt, to enrollment/outcome,
  equivalence-rule revision, reason
Credential: immutable issuance ID, user, course version,
  demonstrated outcomes, issued_at, supersedes (optional)</pre><p>Use immutable published manifests and draft/review/publish states. Promote content, exercise data, tool payloads and grading together. Add optimistic concurrency or an append-only event ledger and idempotency keys for completion/XP. Index active enrollment intentionally; the current user-only progress lookup is not an enrollment model. Key completion messages by credential/enrollment version: the existing once-per-user email flag would otherwise suppress a future bridge celebration.</p>
<h3>Learner-facing copy</h3><blockquote>Vandalizer 5.0 training is available. Your completed work is saved. You can finish your current course or move to the updated course with eligible credit carried over. Review your remaining requirements before choosing.</blockquote><p>Show “Course version,” last saved lesson, carried-over credit and newly required work in both chat and the panel. Define a supported legacy finish window only after checking actual cohort activity and support capacity; 90 days could be a starting proposal, not an announced deadline. No communications were sent during this review.</p></section>
<section id="delivery"><h2>Implementation sequence and acceptance gates</h2><ol><li><strong>Repair the active experience:</strong> progress serializer/tool contract; mobile header; contrast; Markdown/control copy; grading summary; prerequisite consistency. Acceptance: real tool/service contract tests, 320/390/768/1440 captures, focus/scroll checks and a real local start/resume path.</li><li><strong>Freeze and identify the current course:</strong> snapshot lesson/exercise/rubric definitions; inventory completed, active and unstarted learners; back up progress and dry-run mappings. Report unknown provenance instead of guessing.</li><li><strong>Add enrollment/version support before changing required outcomes:</strong> stable lesson IDs, server cursor, versioned attempts, credit mappings and immutable certificates. Acceptance: old and new cohorts coexist; mid-attempt updates, cross-device resume, duplicate/concurrent submissions and rollback preserve credit.</li><li><strong>Author and assess 5.0:</strong> update each module above, deterministic knowledge checks/diagrams, evidence-bound practical grading and the bridge capstone. Acceptance: a learner can succeed via chat, editor, or a mixed route with equivalent evidence; a deliberately wrong result or unrelated artifact cannot earn credit.</li><li><strong>Pilot and publish:</strong> pilot with a new learner, a halfway learner and a legacy graduate; check completion time, rescue requests and quality of decisions. Publish the new version only after review, then offer cohort-specific choices. Rollback changes the default enrollment version without deleting attempts or credentials.</li></ol><p>Measure course-start failures, actual next-lesson resume, grading disagreement, module abandonment, bridge opt-in/completion and support requests. Instrument by course/enrollment version without logging private document contents.</p></section>
<section id="evidence"><h2>Coverage, verification and limits</h2><p><strong>{len(captures)} captured frontend states</strong> across three selected capture runs (including two repeated completion/check states). All 11 module cards at desktop and 390px; all 74 lesson cards at desktop; targeted lesson/check/completion states at 320/390/768/1440; mobile action reachability; recovery, continuation, provisioning, assessment summary, completion refresh and panel comparison. The gallery is searchable below.</p><p><strong>Automated observations:</strong> {overflow} captures with page-level horizontal overflow; {failing} captures with axe violations; {sum(violations.values())} repeated violation nodes ({esc(str(dict(violations)))}). Repeated nodes across states are not distinct defects. A zero-overflow result does not detect clipped child content, demonstrated by the mobile heading defect.</p><p><strong>Tests:</strong> 44 focused backend tests and 25 frontend tests passed. A separate read-only contract probe reproduced the progress KeyError, false-positive extraction pass and prerequisite bypass. Production Vite build succeeded with existing chunk-size/dynamic-import warnings. The authored panel and exported lesson bodies were compared and match.</p><p><strong>Limits:</strong> isolated local production frontend with synthetic API/NDJSON responses; no production data changed, no real learner enrollment migrated, no external messages sent. This validates rendering and selected browser interactions, not live LLM tutoring, actual lab ingestion/execution, production permissions, full assistive-technology behavior, certificate download bytes or a complete backend-driven graduation. The progress-tool defect independently blocks end-to-end acceptance. At small widths, top and bottom states were inspected for the target long lesson and all module action areas; every lesson was not separately exercised on every device. No dark theme or real 200% browser-zoom sweep is claimed.</p><p>Initial connection/sandbox diagnostics are retained in <code>artifacts/visual-review/certification-2026-10-02</code> and excluded from the product grades. The first supplemental run stopped at a failed completion-refresh assertion; that failure is a reported product observation, not a passing run. A targeted follow-up recorded the stale refresh and captured the remaining states. The selected manifests preserve source fingerprints and fixture limitations. Generated capture files are local ignored artifacts; keep them with this report or regenerate using the checked-in harness.</p><p>Reproduce: build frontend into <code>/private/tmp/vandalizer-cert-qa-build</code>; preview on port 5290; run <code>certification-audit.mjs</code> and <code>certification-interactions.mjs</code> with fresh <code>REVIEW_OUTPUT</code> directories and the locally installed <code>REVIEW_CHROMIUM</code>; run <code>backend/.venv/bin/python frontend/scripts/visual-review/certification-contracts.py</code>. Rebuild this report with <code>python3 frontend/scripts/visual-review/build-certification-audit.py</code>.</p>
<label for="filter">Filter captured states</label><input id="filter" type="search" placeholder="Try: foundations, long-top, check, certified"><p id="count" aria-live="polite"></p>{gallery}</section>
'''
